        <div id="why-sets-disappoint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Cheap Garden Tool Sets Disappoint
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Savings Come From</h3>

          <p>
            The boxed garden tool set is one of the most reliable disappointments in home improvement. The pitch is value: a trowel, a transplanter, a cultivator, a weeder, a pruner, gloves and a tote for less than one quality pruner. The trouble is that the price has to come from somewhere, and it comes from the metal. Most budget sets use thin stamped steel or cast aluminum heads, often joined to the handle with a short tang pressed into a plastic or soft wood grip. That joint is exactly where the force goes when you pry a rock or lever a root.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Budget Sets Fail</h3>

          <p>
            The predictable failures follow. Trowels fold at the neck the first time they meet clay or a buried stone. Cultivator tines splay outward. Painted finishes chip within weeks, and bare steel underneath starts to rust because it was never meant to hold an edge or be oiled. The pruners in these kits are usually anvil designs with soft blades that cannot be sharpened or replaced, so once they start crushing stems rather than cutting them, the only fix is the trash can. Owner reports for these sets tend to describe the same pattern: fine for a few weekends of potting, then one breakage after another.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Set Is Fine</h3>

          <p>
            There are exceptions. Some brands sell small sets of genuinely good tools, and a kit for a child or for a balcony of containers does not need forged steel. But for anyone with beds, shrubs or a vegetable patch, the better approach is to buy fewer, better tools one at a time. The seven tools in this guide, bought individually, will cost more than a boxed set, but they replace the set and the second set you would have bought next spring.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Rule of thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the head and neck are one piece of thick steel, it will probably outlast you.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Look at where the blade meets the handle. A forged tool has a solid, continuous neck that thickens toward the socket or tang. A cheap stamped tool has a thin flat neck, often with a visible crimp or weld. That one detail predicts most failures.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="forged-vs-stamped" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Forged vs Stamped Steel
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Forging and Stamping Differ</h3>

          <p>
            Forged tools are shaped by heating a thick piece of steel and hammering or pressing it into form. The process compresses the metal and lets the maker vary thickness, so a trowel can be thin at the cutting edge and thick at the neck where the stress concentrates. Stamped tools are cut from flat sheet steel, like a cookie cutter, then bent into shape. A stamped head has the same thickness everywhere, which means it is either too thick to slice soil well or too thin to survive prying. Most budget tools choose thin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Forging Allows Harder Steels</h3>

          <p>
            Forging is also what lets a maker use harder steels. DeWit, the Dutch toolmaker, forges its hand tools from boron steel, a carbon steel alloy that hardens well under heat treatment and holds an edge in abrasive soil. Corona forges the body of its ClassicCUT pruners. Felco forges the aluminum handles of the Felco 2, which is why they are covered by a lifetime warranty. In each case, forging is a marker of a tool designed to be used hard and repaired, not replaced.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Stamped Steel Is Acceptable</h3>

          <p>
            Stamped is not always bad. Many good long-handled shovels use stamped heads of thick heat-treated steel, and a well-made stamped spade with a solid socket can last for years. The issue is thin stamped steel combined with a weak joint, which describes most bargain hand tools. When you shop, flex the tool if you can, look at the thickness at the neck, and check whether the maker names the steel. A maker that says only "steel" is usually using the cheapest kind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stainless vs Carbon Steel</h3>

          <p>
            Stainless steel deserves its own note. Stainless resists rust and sheds sticky clay easily, which is why the Barebones hori-hori and many British digging forks use it. The trade-off is that common stainless grades are softer than tool-grade carbon steel, so edges dull faster and need touching up more often. Carbon steel holds an edge longer but rusts if put away wet. Neither is wrong. Pick stainless if you know you will not oil your tools, and carbon steel if you will.
          </p>

