        <div id="bypass-vs-anvil" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Bypass vs Anvil Pruners
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Bypass and Anvil Pruners Cut</h3>

          <p>
            A bypass pruner works like scissors: a sharp upper blade slides past a thicker lower hook, and the stem is sliced between them. An anvil pruner works like a knife on a cutting board: a single blade closes onto a flat metal or plastic anvil. Both cut, but they leave very different wounds. Bypass blades make a clean cut that the plant can seal quickly. Anvil blades compress the stem on the anvil side, and on soft green growth that crushing damages the tissue just below the cut.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Each Design Wins</h3>

          <p>
            For live wood, roses, perennials, fruit trees and shrubs, bypass is the right choice, and every pruner and lopper in this guide is a bypass design. Anvil pruners have one legitimate niche: dead, dry, hard wood, where crushing does no harm and the anvil's leverage makes the cut easier. Some gardeners keep an inexpensive anvil pruner for cutting down dead canes and woody debris. If you own only one pruner, it should be bypass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cutting Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            The other detail to check is cutting capacity, the largest branch diameter the tool is rated for. Both the Felco 2 and the Corona BP 3180 are rated for 1 inch. In practice, most gardeners find that hand pruners are pleasant up to roughly half to three-quarters of their rated capacity, and that forcing them to the limit, or twisting to finish a cut, is how blades get sprung. Anything thicker belongs to the loppers, and the Fiskars PowerGear2 lopper is rated for 2-inch branches. Beyond that, use a pruning saw.
          </p>
        </div>

