          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Smallest Useful First Purchase</h3>

          <p>
            If you have to choose a smaller first purchase, the three most useful tools are the pruner, the hori-hori and gloves. The hori-hori in particular does a surprising share of the trowel's job, because its pointed, concave blade digs planting holes, slices through root balls and pries out dandelions. Many gardeners who buy one find that their trowel sees less use. That said, a proper trowel moves more soil per scoop and is better for filling containers, so it still earns its place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Forget Watering</h3>

          <p>
            Watering is its own category and not really part of the hand-tool kit, but you will need it the day you plant. A long-lasting <a href="/best-garden-hose/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">garden hose</a> with a decent <a href="/best-garden-hose-nozzle/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">hose nozzle</a> handles beds and lawns, and a <a href="/best-watering-can/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">good watering can</a> is the gentler option for seedlings, containers and anything near the house where dragging a hose is a chore.
          </p>
