        <div id="digging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Spades, Shovels and Digging Forks
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spade, Shovel or Fork</h3>

          <p>
            The terms get used loosely, so here is the practical split. A spade has a flatter, more squared blade and is meant for cutting into soil, edging and slicing out sod. A shovel has a curved, pointed bowl designed for scooping and moving loose material. A digging fork has four thick tines and is for loosening compacted soil, lifting root crops and dividing perennials without slicing roots. For a first long-handled tool, a pointed digging shovel with sharp edges does the most jobs, which is why our pick is the Radius Root Slayer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Root Slayer Gets the Pick</h3>

          <p>
            The Root Slayer's distinguishing feature is its serrated edges, which saw through roots rather than bouncing off them. Radius's warranty sheet describes carbon steel blades with saw-tooth edges and its patented O-handle, a large loop grip that you can hold with either hand, gloved or not, at different angles. Retail listings for the standard model give an overall length of about 44.5 inches with a blade around 10.5 inches wide. Radius also sells an XL version with a longer shaft for taller users and more leverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Digging Fork as a Second Tool</h3>

          <p>
            A digging fork is the tool most worth adding second. In heavy clay or compacted soil, a fork penetrates where a spade struggles, and it loosens without inverting soil layers. Spear & Jackson, the British maker, has sold stainless steel digging forks for generations, and its Neverbend line is aimed at heavy digging. Whatever you choose, look for tines that are square or diamond in cross-section rather than flat, since flat tines bend. If you rarely dig new beds, you can skip the fork and use the shovel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hoes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Hoes and Weeding Without Chemicals
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Draw Hoes Are Tiring for Weeding</h3>

          <p>
            A sharp hoe is the most underrated tool in the garden. Used regularly on young weeds, it does most of the work that people otherwise hand to herbicides. The key is choosing a design that slices just below the soil surface rather than chopping into it. Traditional draw hoes, the flat-bladed kind you lift and chop with, are good for breaking ground and moving soil, but they are tiring for weeding and they bring buried weed seeds to the surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stirrup and Dutch Hoes</h3>

          <p>
            Two designs do better for weeding. The stirrup or oscillating hoe has a hinged loop of sharpened steel that cuts on both the push and the pull, so you can work it back and forth like a vacuum. The Dutch hoe has a flat blade angled forward, which you push just under the soil while walking backward, so you never step on the soil you have just weeded. DeWit makes forged versions of both, with carbon steel heads on ash handles. Either beats a draw hoe for weeding.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Timing Matters More Than the Tool</h3>

          <p>
            Timing matters more than the tool. A hoe works best on seedling weeds, ideally on a dry, sunny day when severed weeds wilt quickly. Once weeds are established, with deep taproots like dandelion or dock, you need the hori-hori to lever out the whole root, or the plant regrows from what is left. For hard-to-reach spots such as gravel and paving, our guide on <a href="/how-to-kill-weeds-in-driveway-cracks/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">killing weeds in driveway cracks</a> covers the options that a hoe cannot reach.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Chemistry Still Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            Some situations still call for chemistry, such as a lawn overrun with broadleaf weeds or a fence line choked with poison ivy. If you go that route, read our guide to the <a href="/best-weed-killer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best weed killers</a> for the differences between selective, non-selective and pre-emergent products, so you do not wipe out the lawn along with the weeds. For beds and vegetable rows, though, a sharp hoe used weekly is cheaper, faster and kinder to the soil.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Keeping Tools Sharp and Rust-Free
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cleaning and Oiling After Use</h3>

          <p>
            Good tools reward a few minutes of care, and the care is simple. At the end of each session, knock off soil and wipe the blade. Sap from pruning builds up quickly on pruner blades and makes them stick, so wipe it off with a rag and a little rubbing alcohol. Carbon steel tools, including the DeWit trowel and the Root Slayer, should be dried before storage. A light film of oil, such as boiled linseed oil or a general purpose tool oil, keeps rust off the steel and the ash handles from drying out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sharpening</h3>

          <p>
            Sharpening is where quality tools pay you back. A pruner with a resharpenable blade can be touched up with a small diamond file or sharpening stone, holding the stone at the original bevel angle and working only the beveled side. Shovels, hoes and trowels benefit from a few strokes with a mill file on the working edge each season; a sharp spade cuts soil and roots with noticeably less effort. Cheap tools rarely have enough steel to make this worthwhile.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Replacing Worn Parts</h3>

          <p>
            Replaceable parts are the other half of the story. Felco sells spare blades, springs and anvil blades for the Felco 2, and the company's own guidance is to change the blade when the blade and counter-blade no longer cross cleanly or the edge is badly damaged. Note what the warranty covers: Felco's lifetime warranty applies to the forged aluminum handles, while blades, springs, grips and shock absorbers are classed as consumable parts you replace as they wear. Corona sells replacement blades for the BP 3180 as well.
          </p>
        </div>

