        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What garden tools does a beginner actually need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Seven covers almost everything: a bypass hand pruner, a hori-hori knife, a hand trowel, a spade or digging shovel, bypass loppers, a hoe and a pair of gloves. Start with the pruner, trowel and gloves for planting and tidying, then add the shovel and hoe when you dig beds, and loppers when shrubs need shaping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are garden tool sets worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not for anyone with real beds or shrubs. Budget sets save money by using thin stamped steel and weak handle joints, so key tools bend or break quickly, and they pad the count with near duplicates while leaving out spades and loppers. Buying a few quality tools individually costs more up front but lasts far longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is forged steel really better than stamped steel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For hand tools that you lever and pry, yes. Forging lets the maker make a thin cutting edge with a thick, strong neck, and it supports harder steels such as boron steel that hold an edge. Stamped tools are uniform sheet metal that tends to bend at the neck. Heavy-gauge stamped shovels can still be durable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a bypass or anvil pruner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bypass, for nearly everyone. Bypass blades slide past each other and make a clean cut that live plants heal quickly. Anvil blades close onto a flat plate and crush soft stems. Anvil pruners have a niche on dead, dry wood, so some gardeners keep a cheap one for cleanup, but your main pruner should be bypass.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Felco 2 worth the price?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If it fits your hand and you prune regularly, most owners say yes. It cuts up to 1 inch, its handles are forged aluminum with a lifetime warranty, and every wearing part can be replaced, so it can last decades. If you prune occasionally, the Corona BP 3180 gives you a forged, resharpenable bypass pruner for much less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a hori-hori used for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A hori-hori is a Japanese garden knife with a pointed, concave blade, usually with one straight and one serrated edge. Gardeners use it to dig planting holes, plant bulbs to a measured depth, cut through root balls and roots, slice twine and bags, and lever out tap-rooted weeds. It often takes over many of a trowel's jobs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I keep garden tools from rusting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean off soil after each use, dry the metal, and wipe on a thin coat of oil, such as boiled linseed oil or a general tool oil, which also protects wooden handles. Store tools hanging or off the floor in a dry place. Before winter, do a full clean, sharpen and oil so carbon steel heads come through storage rust-free.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a boxed set first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most budget sets use thin stamped heads joined to soft handles, and the trowel and cultivator tend to bend at the neck within a season. Buy a good pruner, trowel and gloves individually, then add tools as jobs appear. You will spend more per tool, but less overall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an anvil pruner on live plants</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Anvil pruners crush the stem against a flat plate, which damages living tissue below the cut. They are fine for dead, dry wood. For roses, shrubs, perennials and fruit trees, use a bypass pruner, whose blades slide past each other like scissors for a clean cut.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forcing pruners past their capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twisting a 1-inch pruner through a branch that is too thick springs the blades and ruins the cut. If it does not close comfortably, step up to loppers, which handle up to about 2 inches, and use a pruning saw beyond that. Keep pruners for smaller stems.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting carbon steel tools away wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Forged carbon and boron steel heads hold an edge well but rust if left damp in a shed. Knock off soil, dry the blade, and wipe on a thin film of oil after use. Stainless tools tolerate neglect better but need sharpening more often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the wrong size or hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pruner sized for large hands forces a wide grip that tires smaller hands quickly, and right-handed pruners are awkward for left-handers. Check the maker's hand-size guidance; Felco and others sell compact and left-handed models. Fit matters as much as steel quality.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

